  "development_findings": [
    "SQLite no-follow opening rejects proc-fd symlink paths; resolved directory paths are re-opened/compared to held inodes while SQLite still owns pathname VFS operations.",
    "Coordinator certificate identities use hex DER hashes rather than JWT base64url thumbprints. Both are derived from the authenticated TLS certificate.",
    "A persistent mutex-protected native writer connection avoids per-RPC PRAGMA/sidecar races; every RPC still reads rows inside a fresh transaction.",
    "The optional authority needs its own bounded compiler FIFO and explicit projector target; Result.and_then hid the handler call behind an opaque callback, replaced by an ordinary explicit match.",
    "Negative source tests exposed insufficient presence-only admission checking; both check and write call sites are now required."
  ],
  "checked_scope": [
    "26 new authority obligations within a 292-name Garrett audit with only allowed standard logical axioms; exact peer/operator membership, activation/revocation, reservation/completion and positive witnesses.",
    "Pure modeled histories of actual successful Rust decisions preserve a tombstone and a spent key; they do not prove the native SQL/server trace implements that history.",
    "Native initializer/server and independent Python authority/clients share schema and 35 frozen protocol rows, exact replies/durable state, restarts, roles, wrong peers, framing/type denials, private-path refusal and no automatic replay.",
    "Native/Python brokers run against both authorities, including global revocation, uncertain writes, competing retry names, policy fingerprints and outage behavior.",
